.TH VCHK 1M UniSoft
... %W%
.SH NAME
vchk \- version checkup
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vchk
[
argument
] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Vchk
is a highly specialized form of
.IR make (1)
designed to check and maintain the modes, ownerships, and versions of a
set of files specified in the \fIdescription file\fP.
The description file is essentially a
"photograph" of what a healthy system
(i.e., one with all its components in the correct state) looks like.
It contains a list of pathnames (for both files and directories)
that should exist and have specific protections and contents.
.I Vchk
reads the description file, 
checks each item specified and prints error messages
when a file does not match its description.  
Many problems can be fixed directly by
.IR vchk ,
such as incorrect mode and/or owner and missing link names.
All other problems involve actually replacing a file,  
detected by comparing some combination of checksum, length, and/or
version number (from the description file) with
the value generated from the actual file being checked.
When a file needs to be replaced
.I vchk
invokes the command named by the \s-1REMAKE\s+1
macro (see \s-1MACROS\s+1 below).
.PP
Each
.I argument
is either a definition or an option.
Option arguments begin with the character \- and 
consist of a string of letters (called
.IR flags )
from the set
.nh
.RB \` ADIPSabcdefiklmprstvx \'.
.hy
The
.B f
and 
.B t
flags cause the next argument to be considered specially.  The
.B p
and 
.B P
flags cause the rest of the argument in which they appear to
be considered specially.
Other arguments
are either macro definitions
(i.e., \fIname = string\fP pairs)
or simply strings which are saved as numeric macros.
Briefly, the
.I flags
are as follows:
.TP "\w'\fB\-D\fP\ \ 'u"
.BI \-A sysid 
specifies an alternate 
.I sysid 
rather than using the one
found in 
.BR /etc/sys_id .
.TP
.B \-D
enables debugging messages.
.TP
.B \-I
process control lines only.
.TP
.BI \-P sysid
preprocess the description file;
.I sysid
is optional and is explained below under \s-1PREPROCESSING\s+1.
.TP
.B \-S
suppress printing of non-fatal error messages.
.TP
.B \-a
checks all lines in the description file.  Modifies the
.BR b ,
.BR P ,
and
.B k
options.
.TP
.B \-b
build a description file for the current directory.
.TP
.B \-c
print shell commands to fix the file system.
.TP
.B \-d
suppress re-installation commands and error messages.
.TP
.B \-e
suppress checks for everything but existence.
.TP
.BI \-f " filename "
cause
.I vchk
to read filename instead of
.BR /etc/vchk_tree .
.TP
.B \-i
go interactive:  modifies 
.BR b , 
.BR c , 
and 
.B x 
options.
.TP
.B \-k
perform checksums on files having checksum field.
.TP
.B \-l
suppress listing of files left in directories.
.TP
.B \-m
allow multiple copies of files.
.TP
.BI \-p pw_file
force
.I vchk
to re-evaluate and/or use an alternate password file.
.TP
.B \-r
allow redundant password entries (user \s-1ID\s_1).
.TP
.B \-s
remain silent about trivial problems.
.TP
.B \-v
suppress checking of version numbers.
.TP
.B \-x
execute shell commands to fix the file system (cf. the
.B \-c
option which prints rather than executes).
.SS "DESCRIPTION FILE SYNTAX"
Lines in the description file are either comments, 
control lines, specifications, or
commands.  Control lines provide a simple 
.I ifdef
mechanism for selectively ignoring specification lines.  
Specification lines describe
files and/or directories that need to be checked.  
Commands are not processed by
.I vchk
but (in the spirit of
.IR make (1))
are used when the file specified above them is found to need replacing.
.PP
Several conventions are observed to maintain the readability of the 
description file;
for example, a trailing backslash and all leading white space on the
following line are ignored when processed.
In addition, backslash may be used to delay the expansion
of macros (in macro definitions only) and, as described below, to
alter the evaluation of parentheses and braces in pathnames.
.SS COMMENTS
Comment lines always start with a \`#\'.  
If the second character on a comment
line is also a \`#\', then that line is printed on the standard error when
read by
.IR vchk .
Any line may have a trailing comment, which is universally ignored.
.SS "CONTROL LINES"
Control lines start with a \`.\' (period).
The mechanism is similar to the C language pre-processor except that
defined words do not have values associated with them; words are simply
defined or not.
The control commands supported are as follows:
.TP .3i
.IR \&.define " wordlist"
where
.I wordlist
is a list of white-space-separated words to be defined which have
no associated values.
Note that only the first twelve
letters of defined words are significant.  
Storage for defined words is static. 
There is a maximum of sixty defined words at any given time.
.TP .3i
.IR \&.ifdef " define_expression"
where
.I define_expression
is an infix boolean expression involving defined words and the
operators !, &, and |\|, which mean \`not\', \`and\', and \`or\', respectively.
The value of each word evaluates to a boolean "yes" if that word is defined
and "no" if not.  
If the expression evaluates to be false, lines are ignored until
the matching
.IR \&.endif " or " \&.else
control line is read.  
.I Ifndef
is also supported and reverses the sense of the expression test.
.TP
.IR \&.include " filename"
is very similar the C pre-processor 
.I include 
with the exception that
there are no default searching places and that the filename is not
enclosed in double quotes or angle brackets.  In addition, if the first
character of the filename is an exclamation mark (!), then the rest of
line is considered to be a shell command and its standard output is
what gets included.
.TP
.IR \&.undef " wordlist"
undefines each word in the wordlist.
.TP
.IR \&.unset " wordlist"
frees the storage associated with macro definitions (detailed in a
following section) for the given wordlist (which is composed of macro names).  
Words in wordlist that
are already unset (or were never set) are silently ignored.
.TP
.IR \&.chdir " directory_name"
changes the current directory to the one specified and
alters the starting location of pathnames anchored from the current directory. 
.TP
.IR \&.exit " message"
causes
.I vchk
to print the message and exit immediately.
.PP
As an aid to debugging the description file, 
a single \`.\' on a line by itself causes
.I vchk
to print the currently defined control symbols on the
standard error when it reads that line.
.SS MACROS
A macro processing facility very similar to the one used by
.IR make (1)
is provided.
Macros are defined when a line containing the macro name, an equal sign,
and the value is read.  The value may be null or include macro invocations.
Unlike \`defined\' words, macro names are fully significant and
are saved in dynamic memory.
Macros are invoked by the \`$\' character.  As in
.I make
scripts, macro names must be surrounded by \fB()\fPs when they are 
longer than a single letter.
There is a special macro (named \`.\', thus referenced with \`$.\')
which always expands to the name of the current directory.
It is useful in the construction of link names since most files have
their links close by.
.PP
Except in the definition of a new macro (where interpretation may need to
be delayed) and in comments, it is always an error for a macro to be
used if it is undefined.  
Since the \`##\' comment is printed after
macro substitution, it is a useful debugging tool.  
In keeping with the
spirit of the \`dump control words\' command (.), a single \`$\' on a
line by itself prints all the currently defined macros and their
values.
.PP
Note that lines are re-scanned once a macro has been substituted
so that a macro may be defined to expand to a control line, comment,
or even a macro definition.  Note that this degenerates to a recursive
loop if the definition of a macro contains a reference to that macro.
.PP
Two predefined macros exist.  The first, called \s-1REMAKE\s+1, contains the
name (and options) of the program to use to replace damaged or missing files.
If the file 
.B /etc/sys_id
exists and is not empty, it is assumed to contain the
.I UniSoft
code name assigned to your system and
.I vchk
will setup the \s-1REMAKE\s+1 macro to be the
command "take\ \-i\fBN\fP" where \fBN\fP is the name
found in 
.BR /etc/sys_id .
This allows systems that reside at
.I UniSoft
to be updated automatically over a direct 
.I tty 
line via the
.IR take (1M)
program.
If the 
.B /etc/sys_id
file has a single empty line in it, then \s-1REMAKE\s+1
will be set to "take \-i".
This allows remote systems to be updated automatically
over phone lines.  If the 
.B /etc/sys_id
file does not exist,
.I vchk
sets the \s-1REMAKE\s+1 macro to be "install".
Note that the description file may redefine
the \s-1REMAKE\s+1 macro at any time.
.PP
The second predefined macro is called \s-1ARGS\s+1 and can be set
but not referenced.
Strings assigned to \s-1ARGS\s+1 are treated as command line options.
The \- preceding option keyletters is still required,
and enables that option.
A plus sign, \`+\', must be used instead to disable a keyletter option.
Resetting the
.BR b , 
.BR p , 
and 
.B r
is not allowed.
.SS "DESCRIPTION LINES"
Each line that is not a comment, control line, macro definition, or
command is considered to be the specification for a particular file or
directory.  These have a simple and regular
syntax:  the first and only mandatory field is the
.IR pathname ,
which must begin at the root (\fB/\fP) or the current directory (\fB./\fP).  
In practice
we find that starting all lines with a macro allows easy relocation
of the entire tree described and is very readable.
.PP
The rest of the line contains optional information about the contents
and protection of the file.  Contents specifications are separated
from the pathname by white space.  The entire protection specification
is bracketed to separate it from the rest of the line.
.SS PATHNAMES
Pathnames refer to directories (if and only if they end in a \`/\' character) 
or files (if they do not end in a \`/\').
Use of shell metacharacters
.RI ( globbing )
is not supported but two mechanisms are provided to allow variable 
pathnames:  braces, \fB{}\fPs, and parentheses.
Braces are interpreted just as in
.IR csh (1);
each of the expanded pathnames must exist and must match the description
given.
Parentheses in pathnames are interpreted similarly,
except that
.I exactly one
of the resulting pathnames must exist.
This feature is useful, for example, to allow a program to be in
either
.B /bin
or
.BR /usr/bin ,
but not both.
.PP
Parentheses and braces are expanded left to right; for example,
the construction
.B (a,b){x,y}
means either
.B ax
and
.B ay
must exist or
.B bx
and
.BR by .
Backslashes may be used to delay or prevent the interpretation of \fB()\fPs
and \fB{}\fPs.
For example,
.B \e(a,b\e){x,y}
means one of
.B ax
or
.B bx
and one of
.B ay
or
.B by
must exist.  One layer of backslashes are removed for each pass through
the pathname and each time an unescaped parenthesis or bracket is detected
and expanded, another pass is made.
.PP
Note that when alternative paths are used (i.e., parentheses occur in
the pathname) the first one is considered the one to be rebuilt in the
event that all are missing.  For example, the pathname
.B (/usr)/bin/ls
would look first in
.B /usr/bin
for \`ls\', then in
.BR /bin ,
and try to "\s-1REMAKE\s+1\ /usr/bin/ls" if both are missing.
The reverse is true for
.BR (,/usr)/bin/ls .
.SS SPECIFICATIONS
Two kinds of specifications are implemented.  The first kind deals with
the contents of the file or directory and follows the pathname (separated
from it by white space).  
The second kind deals with the files protection; these are enclosed
in some type of parentheses to separate them from contents specifications.
The kind of parentheses used, \fB()\fPs, \fB[]\fPs, \fB<>\fPs,
or \fB{}\fPs, modify the action
taken by
.IR vchk
according to the table below:
.TP
.B ()
Enables checking and replacing of the file.
.TP
.B []
Enables checking but never replacing.  If the file is missing,
.I vchk
will complain but not try to rebuild it.
.TP
.B <>
Enables checking (if and only if the file exists or the
.B \-a
command line option is given) and never replacing.
.TP
.B {}
Enables checking but not repairing, (i.e., if the file is missing then
it will be remade, but if it exists and is incorrect it will not be remade).
.PP
Associated with each directory is a default mode and ownership for 
the files and directories contained within it.
Unless explicitly given, each directory inherits its defaults
from its parent directory.   
If unspecified, the uppermost directory
(either the root or current directory) sets the mode
and ownership of its contents from its own mode and ownership.
These defaults may be reset at any time simply by following the directory
name with a mode and/or user name.
.PP
Regular files have three optional contents specification fields:
length, checksum, and version number.
These may be specified with a word (either Length, Checksum, or Version), 
an optional space, or a numeric value.  
The word may be any prefix, for example, \`Length 34\'
or \`L34\'.  The checksums used are the same as those produced by
.IR sum (1).
The length checked is that returned by
.IR stat (2).
These checks do not apply to device files (only block and character
devices are supported); thus their contents specification field must begin
with either
.B b
or
.B c
and must be followed by the major and minor device number (separated by
white space).  
If
.B x
is used instead of either the major or minor device number,
.I vchk
will allow the device to have any value.
.PP
The protection specification consists of a list of command prefixes
separated by semicolons.  The commands supported are
.I chmod,
.I chown,
and
.IR "link to" .
If angle brackets (i.e., \fB<>\fPs) are used instead of parentheses to
enclose the protection specification, the file or directory so referenced
is optional and will not generate diagnostics if it is missing.  It can
be raised to the status of \fB[]\fPs by giving the
.B \-a
command line option.
If square brackets (i.e., \fB[]\fPs) are used, the referenced file cannot
be replaced automatically, as for example, the password file.
If curly brackets (i.e., \fB{}\fPs) are used, the referenced file will
be replaced only if it is missing, not if it exists and is
wrong (according to the description file).
This is useful for files like 
.BR  /etc/termcap .
.PP
Any other information in the protection specification is treated as
a special comment that is printed with error messages about that file.
.SS OPERATION
In order for
.I vchk
to check the ownership of files it must map user 
.SM ID 
numbers onto login
names.  The password file is normally used for this mapping but it is
too expensive to look up each name every time it is used so
.I vchk
creates a temporary file 
.RB ( /etc/vchk_pw )
the first time it is run; whenever its temporary is out of date
with respect to the real password file,
.I vchk
recreates the temporary file.
.PP
In the process of reading the password file
.I vchk
inspects each account and prints diagnostics when it finds questionable
data there.  These messages are warnings or simply situations which bear
reporting; the format of these messages is "Line\ <number>:\ message".
The word "Error:\ " is prepended to the warnings for a particular line if
.I vchk
has decided to ignore that line of the password file.
.PP
The
.BI \-p[ pw_file ]
option is provided to allow users who do not have write permission in 
.B /etc
to use
.IR vchk .
If specified with a filename after it,
.I vchk
will get the saved password information from that file.  
If the file does not exist,
.I vchk
will create it.
Use of the
.B \-p
option without a filename informs
.I vchk
to reprocess the password file even if it is not out of date.
.PP
.I Vchk
normally expects the description file to be
.BR /etc/vchk_tree ,
but if the standard input to
.I vchk
is a regular file, that file will be read instead.
.PP
Instead of redirecting the standard input, the
.B \-f
option can be used 
to respecify the description file.  It is an error to use both.
.PP
The best way to build a new description file is to
.I chdir 
to the appropriate directory
and run
.I vchk
with the
.B \-b
option.  
A description file for the current directory will be 
produced on the standard output.
The
.B \-i
option may also be used, causing
.I vchk
to ask before descending each directory.
.PP
In addition to reporting errors, the 
.B \-c
flag prints shell commands to correct the detected error.
The
.B \-i
option can be used with the 
.B \-c 
option to ask before outputting a command.
.PP
It is inadvisable to use the
.B \-x
flag until the description file has been used and debugged.
This flag allows
.I vchk
to execute the
.IR chmod (2),
.IR chown (2),
and
.IR ln (2)
commands internally, saving much time.
Re-installation commands (cf. the \s-1REMAKE\s+1 macro) are executed via the
.IR system (3)
call.
.SS PREPROCESSING
The
.BI \-P sysid
command line option provides a means for simplifying a complex 
description file.
Everything except macro substitution is suppressed and after each line
is parsed, it is printed on the standard output instead of being used
to check the filesystem.
.PP
If a
.I sysid
is given after the
.B \-P
flag, then it is used to lookup a line from the
.RB ( /etc/takelist )
file.  (See
.IR take (1m)
for a more complete description of the function of the
.B /etc/takelist
file.)
.PP
The lines in
.B /etc/takelist
are composed of any number of fields (called alternates) separated by
colons (\fB:\fPs).
The first alternate in a line is a list of system names separated by
or bars (\fB|\fP\|s).
The
.I sysid
above is compared with each of the system names in the first alternate
of each line until it is found.  If not found, then
.I vchk
exits with an appropriate error message.
.PP
When the line from
.B /etc/takelist
for the current
.I sysid
is found, then each of the additional alternates are considered lists
of root directories (separated by colons or bars) to be prepended to filenames
in the tree file before looking for them.
.PP
If a file is found in more or less than exactly 1 place in the list,
then an error is reported and that line is not include in the preprocessed
output.  If it is found, then the checksum, length, and version number
are computed from that file and replaced in the preprocessed output.
.SH EXAMPLE
Following are some excerpts from a typical description file.
.RS .3i
.nf
.ta \w'$B/sccsdif\ \ \ 'u +\w'C54686 L1253\ \ \ 'u
B = (/usr)/bin	# programs can be in /bin or /usr/bin
.sp .5
/	bin 755	(chmod 755)
$B/ar	Version 1.0
$B/awk	Version 1.3
$B/more	Version 1.0	(link to $./page)
$B/sccsdiff	C54686 L1253	(shell script)	
$B/su	Version 1.0	(chown root; chmod 4755)
/etc/	root 644
/etc/passwd		[password file]
/etc/group		[group file]
/etc/init	Version 1.0	(chmod 700)
/etc/update	Version 1.1	(chmod 700)
/etc/ddate		<dump dates>
.fi
.RE
.PP
The first line of the above example defines a macro,
.IR B ,
to be the string 
.BR (/usr)/bin .
This macro is then invoked on lines 3 through
7 of the example to allow the programs mentioned to be in either 
.B /usr/bin 
or
.BR /bin .
.PP
The second line specifies that the root directory 
.RB ( / ) should have mode 755 and
that the default mode and owner for files found in it be 755 and 
.BR bin.
.PP
The third line specifies that the 
.I ar
program should be version 1.0, owned
by 
.B bin
and have mode 755.  The mode and owner are implied in the following
way.  Each directory inherits its mode and ownership from its parent.  Thus
.B /bin 
inherits the owner of root (which is unspecified in the example and
thus defaults to whatever the owner of the root 
.RB ( / )
is when the example is run).
The mode of the root directory is specified as 755.
.SH FILES
.TP "\w'<standard output>\ \ 'u"
/etc/vchk_pw	
the file where
.I vchk
saves the password file summary.
.TP
/etc/passwd
the password file.
.TP
/etc/vchk_tree
the default description file.
.TP
/dev/tty
where
.I vchk
prints questions and gets the responses (when the
.B \-i
option is used).
.TP
<standard error>
used to print all diagnostics.
.TP
<standard output>
used to print shell commands and the newly built description file 
(when using the
.B \-b
option).
.TP
<standard input>
considered the default description file if it is a regular file.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
chmod(1), ln(1), chown(1M).
.SH BUGS
There is no way (except tediously via the
.B \-i
option) to exclude directories from inspection when building a new 
description file.
There is also no way to automatically update an existing 
description file (i.e., to tell
.I vchk
to fix the description file instead of the filesystem).
.\"	@(#)vchk.1m	5.2 of 7/26/84
